1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,208
Computational Skepticism, Exam Prep.

2
00:00:01,258 --> 00:00:02,780
Key Terms: The Skeptic's Toolkit.

3
00:00:02,830 --> 00:00:07,183
Twenty-five terms for checking what an AI
system says before you trust it, from

4
00:00:07,233 --> 00:00:09,384
measure versus want to the prediction-
lock.

5
00:00:09,434 --> 00:00:14,101
Knowing each exact term, and its look-
alike, lets you name a failure when you

6
00:00:14,151 --> 00:00:14,730
see one.

7
00:00:15,880 --> 00:00:16,070
Hallo.

8
00:00:16,120 --> 00:00:17,513
This is Liam, in for Bear.

9
00:00:17,563 --> 00:00:20,880
A newcomer believes that if an AI output
looks right, you can trust it.

10
00:00:20,930 --> 00:00:22,563
But looking right is polish, not proof.

11
00:00:22,613 --> 00:00:26,651
If the output looks right, you still have
to check it, and checking is a set of

12
00:00:26,701 --> 00:00:27,613
moves you can learn.

13
00:00:27,663 --> 00:00:31,220
This film goes through the terms for those
moves, each one shown in a picture.

14
00:00:31,570 --> 00:00:34,542
Six labels sit in the corners of the
pictures that follow.

15
00:00:34,592 --> 00:00:37,839
In the book text: the course text uses and
explains the term.

16
00:00:37,889 --> 00:00:42,509
In the glossary: the term is on the course
term list, but the text does not explain

17
00:00:42,559 --> 00:00:42,783
it.

18
00:00:42,833 --> 00:00:45,805
Computed: the numbers are worked out on
screen from stated inputs.

19
00:00:45,855 --> 00:00:50,201
Simulation: a made-up run with a fixed
seed, so it comes out the same every time.

20
00:00:50,251 --> 00:00:52,948
Illustrative data: numbers chosen to show
the idea, not measured.

21
00:00:52,998 --> 00:00:55,970
Conceptual: a drawing of the idea, with no
data behind it.

22
00:00:56,320 --> 00:01:00,370
This table is a map of the film, filled
one row at a time.

23
00:01:00,420 --> 00:01:04,471
Twenty-five terms, all about checking what
an AI system says before you trust it.

24
00:01:04,521 --> 00:01:08,571
The first group, Measure and want, holds
measure versus want, gap from the start,

25
00:01:08,621 --> 00:01:11,500
gap that opens later, Goodhart's law and
held-out test set.

26
00:01:11,850 --> 00:01:17,425
The second group is Four moves, what to do
to a claim before trusting it.

27
00:01:17,475 --> 00:01:21,550
Its terms are skepticism, Cartesian doubt,
falsifiability, rhetorical claim, limit of

28
00:01:21,600 --> 00:01:25,300
induction, distribution shift, drift,
artifact versus world and Kantian slice.

29
00:01:25,650 --> 00:01:29,031
The third group is Who does the checking.

30
00:01:29,081 --> 00:01:33,321
It opens with solve-verify asymmetry, then
the supervisor's five capacities,

31
00:01:33,371 --> 00:01:36,323
plausibility auditing, problem
formulation, interpretive judgment, tool

32
00:01:36,373 --> 00:01:41,470
orchestration and executive integration,
plus one hard call named no tool yet.

33
00:01:41,820 --> 00:01:47,020
The last group is Fluency and practice,
why polish misleads and how to build in

34
00:01:47,070 --> 00:01:47,720
the check.

35
00:01:47,770 --> 00:01:51,570
Its terms are fluency trap, form versus
content, institutional skepticism and

36
00:01:51,620 --> 00:01:51,920
prediction-lock.

37
00:01:51,970 --> 00:01:56,120
The exam expects the exact term, so say
each one as written.

38
00:01:56,470 --> 00:01:59,366
Two systems, what each measured beside
what anyone cared about.

39
00:01:59,416 --> 00:02:03,489
Measure versus want means the scored
number only stands in for the real need.

40
00:02:03,539 --> 00:02:06,435
Netflix scored rating error and needed
something to watch tonight.

41
00:02:06,485 --> 00:02:08,497
Amazon scored résumé resemblance and
needed ability.

42
00:02:08,547 --> 00:02:12,032
For example, a job seeker counts the
applications she sends each week.

43
00:02:12,082 --> 00:02:16,450
What she wants is a good offer, and the
count only stands in for it.

44
00:02:16,800 --> 00:02:18,167
Forty simulated candidates, as dots.

45
00:02:18,217 --> 00:02:21,284
Gap from the start means the stand-in
never matched the target.

46
00:02:21,334 --> 00:02:23,268
The correlation is only zero point two.

47
00:02:23,318 --> 00:02:28,085
Of the ten red points, highest by measure,
just two are in the top ten by ability.

48
00:02:28,135 --> 00:02:32,052
For example, a job seeker ranks listings
by how impressive the job title sounds.

49
00:02:32,102 --> 00:02:37,437
A grand title was never the same as a good
job, so the gap was there from the first

50
00:02:37,487 --> 00:02:37,720
listing.

51
00:02:38,070 --> 00:02:41,166
Now the link between measure and want over
time, still simulated.

52
00:02:41,216 --> 00:02:44,597
Gap that opens later means the measure
matched, then the world moved.

53
00:02:44,647 --> 00:02:48,315
The link falls from zero point nine two to
zero point three seven.

54
00:02:48,365 --> 00:02:52,319
For example, recruiters long judged care
by how polished a cover letter was, and

55
00:02:52,369 --> 00:02:53,749
for years polish tracked effort.

56
00:02:53,799 --> 00:02:56,894
Then AI tools let anyone produce a
polished letter in seconds.

57
00:02:56,944 --> 00:03:00,040
The measure stayed the same, and it
stopped tracking the want.

58
00:03:00,390 --> 00:03:02,866
A simulated run, with candidates picked by
the measure.

59
00:03:02,916 --> 00:03:06,514
Goodhart's law says a measure stops being
good once it becomes the target.

60
00:03:06,564 --> 00:03:10,162
At the top two percent the measure, in
red, reaches three point three.

61
00:03:10,212 --> 00:03:12,127
What you want reaches one point five.

62
00:03:12,177 --> 00:03:14,933
For example, applicant-tracking systems
score résumés on keywords from the

63
00:03:14,983 --> 00:03:18,020
listing, so job seekers start copying
those keywords into their résumés.

64
00:03:18,070 --> 00:03:22,230
The scores climb, and they say less and
less about who can do the job.

65
00:03:22,580 --> 00:03:24,288
Bars of prediction error, still simulated.

66
00:03:24,338 --> 00:03:26,926
Training data is what the model was built
from.

67
00:03:26,976 --> 00:03:29,857
A held-out test set is kept back, only for
scoring.

68
00:03:29,907 --> 00:03:34,253
The memorizer has zero error on seen cases
and zero point five nine held out.

69
00:03:34,303 --> 00:03:38,356
For example, a model that predicts who
gets an interview is built from past

70
00:03:38,406 --> 00:03:41,872
applications, while a batch of
applications is locked away where it never

71
00:03:41,922 --> 00:03:42,459
sees them.

72
00:03:42,509 --> 00:03:44,510
Only its score on that batch counts.

73
00:03:44,860 --> 00:03:47,536
Four boxes, one move each from Descartes,
Hume, Popper and Kant.

74
00:03:47,586 --> 00:03:49,518
Skepticism here is a method, not a mood.

75
00:03:49,568 --> 00:03:53,730
It is a set of moves performed on a claim,
and each move leaves something you can

76
00:03:53,780 --> 00:03:53,978
inspect.

77
00:03:54,028 --> 00:03:58,190
For example, a job seeker who says I just
do not trust this résumé scorer shows a

78
00:03:58,240 --> 00:03:58,438
mood.

79
00:03:58,488 --> 00:04:02,402
One who writes down what has changed in
the job market since the scorer was tested

80
00:04:02,452 --> 00:04:04,880
has made a move, and anyone can check the
note.

81
00:04:05,230 --> 00:04:08,070
Eight boxes, Knight Capital's servers on
the day of an update.

82
00:04:08,120 --> 00:04:11,486
Cartesian doubt means doubting a claim
until you can list the conditions under

83
00:04:11,536 --> 00:04:12,799
which it would be wrong.

84
00:04:12,849 --> 00:04:15,164
For example, take the claim that the
deployment succeeded.

85
00:04:15,214 --> 00:04:19,631
It would be wrong if a server missed the
update, and only seven of eight took it.

86
00:04:19,681 --> 00:04:23,046
It would be wrong if the warning went
unseen, and ninety-seven emails went

87
00:04:23,096 --> 00:04:23,309
unopened.

88
00:04:23,359 --> 00:04:24,360
Those are the suspects.

89
00:04:24,710 --> 00:04:26,386
Each circle is twenty-five white swans.

90
00:04:26,436 --> 00:04:30,127
Popperian falsifiability means a claim
says in advance what would prove it false,

91
00:04:30,177 --> 00:04:32,142
a metric, a threshold and a window.

92
00:04:32,192 --> 00:04:34,731
One thousand white swans do not prove the
claim.

93
00:04:34,781 --> 00:04:36,170
One black swan refutes it.

94
00:04:36,220 --> 00:04:40,486
For example, the maker of an AI job agent
says it finds you relevant listings.

95
00:04:40,536 --> 00:04:44,227
The falsifiable version names the share of
listings you reject as irrelevant, the

96
00:04:44,277 --> 00:04:47,680
line it must stay under, and the month it
is checked over.

97
00:04:48,030 --> 00:04:50,661
Twelve made-up error bars and a two
percent line.

98
00:04:50,711 --> 00:04:53,343
A rhetorical claim is one no observation
could contradict.

99
00:04:53,393 --> 00:04:56,024
Saying the system is robust fits all
twelve outcomes.

100
00:04:56,074 --> 00:05:00,791
Saying below two percent in every window
is a test, and the red bar fails it.

101
00:05:00,841 --> 00:05:04,367
For example, a résumé service promises to
make your résumé stand out.

102
00:05:04,417 --> 00:05:08,240
No number of callbacks, high or low, could
ever show that promise false.

103
00:05:08,590 --> 00:05:12,250
An illustration, confidence that some
Python gets an entry-level job, over ten

104
00:05:12,300 --> 00:05:12,868
good years.

105
00:05:12,918 --> 00:05:16,578
Limit of induction, Hume's move, means
past successes guarantee nothing about the

106
00:05:16,628 --> 00:05:17,196
next case.

107
00:05:17,246 --> 00:05:19,051
Picture someone who knows some Python.

108
00:05:19,101 --> 00:05:23,379
For ten years that was enough for an
entry-level programming job, and each good

109
00:05:23,429 --> 00:05:27,088
year made it look surer, up to zero point
nine one seven.

110
00:05:27,138 --> 00:05:31,107
Then AI coding agents arrived, the world
changed, and those jobs started going

111
00:05:31,157 --> 00:05:31,416
away.

112
00:05:31,466 --> 00:05:33,580
Ten good years could not show it.

113
00:05:33,930 --> 00:05:36,550
The same model twice, in a simulated run.

114
00:05:36,600 --> 00:05:40,222
Distribution shift means the world after
deployment no longer resembles the

115
00:05:40,272 --> 00:05:40,889
training data.

116
00:05:40,939 --> 00:05:43,560
Before, confidence and accuracy are both
eighty-four percent.

117
00:05:43,610 --> 00:05:46,898
After, the model says eighty-nine percent
and is right sixty-six.

118
00:05:46,948 --> 00:05:50,903
For example, a résumé screener that
learned from experienced engineers is put

119
00:05:50,953 --> 00:05:52,572
to work on new graduates.

120
00:05:52,622 --> 00:05:56,911
Their résumés list class projects instead
of jobs, and it answers just as

121
00:05:56,961 --> 00:05:58,580
confidently while getting more wrong.

122
00:05:58,930 --> 00:06:01,349
Weekly bars of PSI, population stability
index, simulated.

123
00:06:01,399 --> 00:06:05,362
Drift means inputs changing over time from
what the model was built on.

124
00:06:05,412 --> 00:06:08,757
PSI scores how far this week's inputs sit
from a baseline.

125
00:06:08,807 --> 00:06:13,696
Week five is first over the zero point one
line, and raises an alert for human

126
00:06:13,746 --> 00:06:14,004
review.

127
00:06:14,054 --> 00:06:18,017
For example, a model that scores job
listings was built when most listings

128
00:06:18,067 --> 00:06:18,943
named an office.

129
00:06:18,993 --> 00:06:23,881
Week by week more of them say remote, and
the inputs slowly move away from the

130
00:06:23,931 --> 00:06:24,190
baseline.

131
00:06:24,540 --> 00:06:27,121
Three cases, each split into artifact,
world and relationship.

132
00:06:27,171 --> 00:06:31,507
Artifact versus world means the output a
system produces is not the real thing it

133
00:06:31,557 --> 00:06:32,091
should describe.

134
00:06:32,141 --> 00:06:36,184
In the COVID chest X-ray case, the
artifact is a confident label, the world

135
00:06:36,234 --> 00:06:37,354
is the patient's lungs.

136
00:06:37,404 --> 00:06:40,570
For example, a job board marks a listing
as actively hiring.

137
00:06:40,620 --> 00:06:42,032
The label is the artifact.

138
00:06:42,082 --> 00:06:45,540
Whether anyone is still being hired for
that job is the world.

139
00:06:45,890 --> 00:06:47,171
Two bars, with invented rates.

140
00:06:47,221 --> 00:06:51,694
Kantian slice means you only see a slice
of the world, so keep in view what is

141
00:06:51,744 --> 00:06:52,226
outside it.

142
00:06:52,276 --> 00:06:55,952
A model keyed on a corner marker is
ninety-five percent right at its training

143
00:06:56,002 --> 00:06:57,815
hospitals, forty-eight percent at a new
one.

144
00:06:57,865 --> 00:07:01,806
For example, a hiring model only ever sees
how the people it hired turned out.

145
00:07:01,856 --> 00:07:06,064
The people it turned away sit outside its
slice, and no report from it can show

146
00:07:06,114 --> 00:07:06,330
them.

147
00:07:06,680 --> 00:07:08,454
Two bars on a log scale.

148
00:07:08,504 --> 00:07:11,190
Solve means find the answer, verify means
check it.

149
00:07:11,240 --> 00:07:14,533
Solve-verify asymmetry starts in computer
science, where checking is easier than

150
00:07:14,583 --> 00:07:14,837
finding.

151
00:07:14,887 --> 00:07:18,485
Here, finding a factor took five hundred
thousand and one trial divisions.

152
00:07:18,535 --> 00:07:19,701
Checking took one step.

153
00:07:19,751 --> 00:07:23,956
For example, finding a listing that meets
all your requirements can take weeks of

154
00:07:24,006 --> 00:07:27,300
searching, but checking one listing
against them takes a few minutes.

155
00:07:27,650 --> 00:07:30,329
The same comparison for AI deployment,
with made-up durations.

156
00:07:30,379 --> 00:07:32,754
Here the solve-verify asymmetry runs the
other way.

157
00:07:32,804 --> 00:07:35,180
The model produces a report in fifty
milliseconds.

158
00:07:35,230 --> 00:07:37,302
A person verifying it takes one hour.

159
00:07:37,352 --> 00:07:39,728
So verification must be budgeted and
designed in.

160
00:07:39,778 --> 00:07:43,973
For example, an AI agent tailors a résumé
to a listing in a moment.

161
00:07:44,023 --> 00:07:48,521
The job seeker still has to check every
date, title and claim against her real

162
00:07:48,571 --> 00:07:50,340
history, and that takes far longer.

163
00:07:50,690 --> 00:07:53,402
A made-up ranked list of engineers, with
years shipping code.

164
00:07:53,452 --> 00:07:56,992
Plausibility auditing, capacity one, asks
whether an output could be true at all,

165
00:07:57,042 --> 00:07:58,096
given what you know.

166
00:07:58,146 --> 00:08:01,410
The four most experienced, in red, all sit
in the bottom half.

167
00:08:01,460 --> 00:08:02,791
That twitch is the audit.

168
00:08:02,841 --> 00:08:06,658
For example, an AI salary tool says an
internship pays more than the manager's

169
00:08:06,708 --> 00:08:07,486
job above it.

170
00:08:07,536 --> 00:08:10,800
Anyone who knows the field feels the
twitch before checking any data.

171
00:08:11,150 --> 00:08:13,637
Two rows of simulated patients, six
flagged in each.

172
00:08:13,687 --> 00:08:16,737
Problem formulation, capacity two, means
checking that the system answers the

173
00:08:16,787 --> 00:08:17,582
question you need.

174
00:08:17,632 --> 00:08:20,683
Ranked by cost, one of the six is from
group B.

175
00:08:20,733 --> 00:08:22,092
Ranked by sickness, four are.

176
00:08:22,142 --> 00:08:26,601
For example, a job seeker tells an AI
agent to find listings that match her past

177
00:08:26,651 --> 00:08:30,547
job titles, when what she needs is the job
she can grow into next.

178
00:08:30,597 --> 00:08:32,520
Those two questions point at different
listings.

179
00:08:32,870 --> 00:08:36,109
Two illustrative grids of one hundred,
five red in each.

180
00:08:36,159 --> 00:08:39,727
Interpretive judgment, capacity four,
means reading a number in its domain.

181
00:08:39,777 --> 00:08:44,002
For example, five good emails in the junk
folder is a small nuisance.

182
00:08:44,052 --> 00:08:47,949
Five healthy people told they may have
cancer is a serious harm.

183
00:08:47,999 --> 00:08:50,580
Same five percent false positive rate,
different meaning.

184
00:08:50,930 --> 00:08:53,803
Four sub-problems, each sent to a tool, as
an illustration.

185
00:08:53,853 --> 00:08:57,018
Tool orchestration, capacity three, means
choosing which tool fits which sub-

186
00:08:57,068 --> 00:08:57,311
problem.

187
00:08:57,361 --> 00:09:01,695
Résumé skills go to AI, an unusual role to
human judgment, the arithmetic to a

188
00:09:01,745 --> 00:09:04,033
different model, and one gets no tool yet.

189
00:09:04,083 --> 00:09:08,710
For example, a job seeker lets an AI agent
gather listings, checks the salary math in

190
00:09:08,760 --> 00:09:12,510
a spreadsheet, and asks a former colleague
what the team is really like.

191
00:09:12,860 --> 00:09:15,942
Sixty simulated past titles, with a ring
around each show's comparables.

192
00:09:15,992 --> 00:09:20,212
No tool yet is the decision to deploy
nothing, because nobody knows how to do

193
00:09:20,262 --> 00:09:21,351
the task well enough.

194
00:09:21,401 --> 00:09:24,198
The familiar show has nineteen
comparables, the unprecedented one has

195
00:09:24,248 --> 00:09:24,483
none.

196
00:09:24,533 --> 00:09:29,323
For example, a job seeker is offered a
tool that estimates pay for a kind of role

197
00:09:29,373 --> 00:09:31,316
that did not exist until this year.

198
00:09:31,366 --> 00:09:35,871
With nothing comparable to learn from, the
right call is no tool yet, and she asks

199
00:09:35,921 --> 00:09:37,010
people doing the work.

200
00:09:37,360 --> 00:09:40,231
Three outputs feed one decision with a
named owner.

201
00:09:40,281 --> 00:09:43,801
Executive integration, capacity five,
means someone turns the outputs of tools,

202
00:09:43,851 --> 00:09:47,046
models and people into one decision and
stands behind it.

203
00:09:47,096 --> 00:09:48,994
Tool orchestration only picks the tools.

204
00:09:49,044 --> 00:09:52,888
For example, the applicant-tracking system
ranks a candidate low, a skills test

205
00:09:52,938 --> 00:09:55,485
scores her high, and the interviewer says
hire.

206
00:09:55,535 --> 00:09:58,730
The hiring manager makes the call and
answers for it.

207
00:09:59,080 --> 00:10:01,454
Two chains of boxes, the trap and the
interruption.

208
00:10:01,504 --> 00:10:05,495
Fluency trap means smooth output makes you
trust the answer, and then trust your own

209
00:10:05,545 --> 00:10:06,303
judgment of it.

210
00:10:06,353 --> 00:10:07,380
Fluent is not correct.

211
00:10:07,430 --> 00:10:09,804
Interrupt the trap by writing the failure
condition first.

212
00:10:09,854 --> 00:10:14,383
For example, a job seeker asks an AI for a
cover letter and gets a smooth, confident

213
00:10:14,433 --> 00:10:14,653
page.

214
00:10:14,703 --> 00:10:18,693
The polish makes it feel checked, so she
sends it without noticing it claims a

215
00:10:18,743 --> 00:10:20,040
skill she does not have.

216
00:10:20,390 --> 00:10:24,197
Four sentences, each with the same grey
bar for how well it reads.

217
00:10:24,247 --> 00:10:28,053
Form versus content means smooth wording
and truth are independent in AI output.

218
00:10:28,103 --> 00:10:32,800
For example, two of them say Paris is the
capital of France, and the Eiffel Tower

219
00:10:32,850 --> 00:10:34,283
was completed in nineteen twenty-five.

220
00:10:34,333 --> 00:10:35,470
Both sound equally smooth.

221
00:10:35,520 --> 00:10:39,030
Only the first is true, and the bars
cannot tell you which.

222
00:10:39,380 --> 00:10:43,182
Two rows of thirty days, filled dots for
days the check was done.

223
00:10:43,232 --> 00:10:46,738
Institutional skepticism means writing the
moves into the checklist and workflow, not

224
00:10:46,788 --> 00:10:47,923
leaving them to memory.

225
00:10:47,973 --> 00:10:50,886
In this made-up example, memory checks
twenty-one days of thirty.

226
00:10:50,936 --> 00:10:52,367
The workflow checks all thirty.

227
00:10:52,417 --> 00:10:56,219
For example, a recruiting team writes the
question, what would make this ranking

228
00:10:56,269 --> 00:10:57,701
wrong, into the review form.

229
00:10:57,751 --> 00:11:00,960
Every reviewer answers it on every batch,
whoever is on shift.

230
00:11:01,310 --> 00:11:04,373
A locked prediction, an appended
observation and a gap analysis.

231
00:11:04,423 --> 00:11:07,485
Prediction-lock means writing your
prediction before seeing the result and

232
00:11:07,535 --> 00:11:08,419
never revising it.

233
00:11:08,469 --> 00:11:12,466
In this illustration the prediction was
nine of ten correct, the observation seven

234
00:11:12,516 --> 00:11:13,088
of ten.

235
00:11:13,138 --> 00:11:17,757
For example, before an AI agent sends a
week of applications, a job seeker writes

236
00:11:17,807 --> 00:11:19,625
down how many interviews she expects.

237
00:11:19,675 --> 00:11:23,360
Afterward she adds the real count below
and never edits the guess.

238
00:11:23,710 --> 00:11:26,062
Two ideas people mix up, side by side.

239
00:11:26,112 --> 00:11:30,264
Artifact is the output the system
produces, here two stars, a number on a

240
00:11:30,314 --> 00:11:30,564
screen.

241
00:11:30,614 --> 00:11:34,767
World is the real thing that output should
speak about, whether an imagined applicant

242
00:11:34,817 --> 00:11:35,968
can do the job.

243
00:11:36,018 --> 00:11:39,270
The dashed link between them is to be
inspected, not assumed.

244
00:11:39,620 --> 00:11:43,227
On the left a list, on the right a bar
crossing a line.

245
00:11:43,277 --> 00:11:46,884
Cartesian doubt lists what could be wrong,
such as training data that under-

246
00:11:46,934 --> 00:11:47,728
represents the applicant.

247
00:11:47,778 --> 00:11:48,853
Those are the suspects.

248
00:11:48,903 --> 00:11:52,510
Falsifiability names the observation that
would show it, a metric over the line.

249
00:11:52,560 --> 00:11:54,760
It is a test you could see fail.

250
00:11:55,110 --> 00:11:59,080
On the left a ranking, on the right one
number in two settings.

251
00:11:59,130 --> 00:12:02,482
Plausibility auditing asks whether the
output could be true at all.

252
00:12:02,532 --> 00:12:04,647
Strong people at the bottom looks odd.

253
00:12:04,697 --> 00:12:07,121
Interpretive judgment asks what a number
means here.

254
00:12:07,171 --> 00:12:11,141
Five percent false positives is acceptable
for a spam filter, catastrophic for a

255
00:12:11,191 --> 00:12:11,760
cancer screen.

256
00:12:12,110 --> 00:12:15,468
Two small lines, one flat and low, one
high then falling.

257
00:12:15,518 --> 00:12:19,805
Gap from the start is a stand-in that
never matched, caught before deployment by

258
00:12:19,855 --> 00:12:21,354
asking what the number measures.

259
00:12:21,404 --> 00:12:25,691
Gap that opens later is a match that
decayed when things changed, caught only

260
00:12:25,741 --> 00:12:26,620
by checking again.

261
00:12:26,970 --> 00:12:31,752
On the left a vague claim, on the right a
made-up chart of what its exact version

262
00:12:31,802 --> 00:12:32,036
checks.

263
00:12:32,086 --> 00:12:36,015
The claim that the model performs well
becomes precision at least zero point nine

264
00:12:36,065 --> 00:12:39,995
one and recall at least zero point eight
eight on the held-out set, checked

265
00:12:40,045 --> 00:12:40,279
monthly.

266
00:12:40,329 --> 00:12:41,700
Three of six months fail.

267
00:12:42,050 --> 00:12:45,614
The claim that the system is robust
becomes error on out-of-distribution

268
00:12:45,664 --> 00:12:49,556
inputs, those unlike the training data,
below two percent over a thirty-day

269
00:12:49,606 --> 00:12:50,213
rolling window.

270
00:12:50,263 --> 00:12:54,483
In the made-up chart the average first
reaches two percent on day eighty.

271
00:12:54,533 --> 00:12:56,454
The rhetorical claim could never fail.

272
00:12:56,504 --> 00:12:57,440
This one can.

273
00:12:57,790 --> 00:13:02,226
The claim that we have mitigated drift
becomes PSI below zero point one on the

274
00:13:02,276 --> 00:13:06,413
top three input features, week over week,
with an alert and review if breached.

275
00:13:06,463 --> 00:13:10,600
In the made-up chart, two feature-weeks
cross the line and would raise an alert.

276
00:13:10,950 --> 00:13:15,860
The claim that the migration was a success
becomes zero data-loss events by row

277
00:13:15,910 --> 00:13:19,403
counts, and ninety-fifth percentile
latency at most one hundred twenty

278
00:13:19,453 --> 00:13:20,820
milliseconds at forty-eight hours.

279
00:13:20,870 --> 00:13:26,134
In this made-up check, one of four tables
lost thirteen rows, and latency is one

280
00:13:26,184 --> 00:13:27,197
hundred thirty-seven milliseconds.

281
00:13:27,247 --> 00:13:28,260
It fails twice.

282
00:13:28,610 --> 00:13:32,154
The claim, I checked it and it looks
right, becomes a procedure.

283
00:13:32,204 --> 00:13:36,348
Write what a wrong answer looks like
before reading, then mark pass, fail or

284
00:13:36,398 --> 00:13:36,647
unknown.

285
00:13:36,697 --> 00:13:41,140
Of ten made-up answers that all looked
right, six pass, two fail and two are

286
00:13:41,190 --> 00:13:41,440
unknown.

287
00:13:41,790 --> 00:13:46,134
A checklist of six questions to ask before
you trust an AI system's output, ticked

288
00:13:46,184 --> 00:13:47,306
one at a time.

289
00:13:47,356 --> 00:13:48,477
First, measure versus want.

290
00:13:48,527 --> 00:13:51,992
Ask what the system measures, and what its
builders actually care about.

291
00:13:52,042 --> 00:13:53,750
They are not the same thing.

292
00:13:54,100 --> 00:13:55,704
Second, the two kinds of gap.

293
00:13:55,754 --> 00:13:59,839
Ask whether this is a gap from the start,
or a gap that opens later.

294
00:13:59,889 --> 00:14:03,699
Then ask when you last checked again,
because a measure that matched once can

295
00:14:03,749 --> 00:14:04,250
stop matching.

296
00:14:04,600 --> 00:14:06,364
Third, Cartesian doubt and falsifiability
together.

297
00:14:06,414 --> 00:14:11,503
Ask what would have to be true for this to
be wrong, and what observation would show

298
00:14:11,553 --> 00:14:11,805
it.

299
00:14:11,855 --> 00:14:15,130
The first question gives the suspects, the
second gives the test.

300
00:14:15,480 --> 00:14:16,837
Fourth, artifact versus world.

301
00:14:16,887 --> 00:14:21,059
Name the artifact, name the world, and
name the relationship between them.

302
00:14:21,109 --> 00:14:25,280
Remember the Kantian slice, any check sees
only part of the world.

303
00:14:25,630 --> 00:14:28,880
Fifth, the supervisory capacities,
plausibility auditing, problem

304
00:14:28,930 --> 00:14:32,180
formulation, tool orchestration,
interpretive judgment and executive

305
00:14:32,230 --> 00:14:32,651
integration.

306
00:14:32,701 --> 00:14:38,780
Ask who exercises each capacity here, and
whether any is left to nobody.

307
00:14:39,130 --> 00:14:40,400
Sixth, the fluency trap.

308
00:14:40,450 --> 00:14:44,360
Ask whether you wrote the failure
condition before reading, and used a

309
00:14:44,410 --> 00:14:44,690
prediction-lock.

310
00:14:44,740 --> 00:14:46,010
Then say it exactly.

311
00:14:46,060 --> 00:14:47,330
Not, it performs well.

312
00:14:47,380 --> 00:14:51,290
Instead, recall at least zero point eight
eight on the held-out set.

313
00:14:52,140 --> 00:14:52,648
Let's recap.

314
00:14:52,698 --> 00:14:56,278
A measure is a stand-in for what you want,
not the want itself.

315
00:14:56,328 --> 00:14:59,907
Skepticism is a method, a set of moves
that leave something to inspect.

316
00:14:59,957 --> 00:15:03,258
The artifact is the output, the world is
what it should describe.

317
00:15:03,308 --> 00:15:06,049
In AI deployment, producing is cheap and
verifying is costly.

318
00:15:06,099 --> 00:15:09,679
The supervisory capacities belong to a
human, and someone must own the decision.

319
00:15:09,729 --> 00:15:12,750
Fluent is not correct, so write the
failure condition before reading.

320
00:15:13,100 --> 00:15:14,951
Quiz me on the skeptic's toolkit.

321
00:15:15,001 --> 00:15:18,754
Give me one short case at a time about an
AI system.

322
00:15:18,804 --> 00:15:23,508
I must name the exact term, choosing from
measure versus want, gap from the start,

323
00:15:23,558 --> 00:15:26,677
gap that opens later, Cartesian doubt,
falsifiability, rhetorical claim, artifact

324
00:15:26,727 --> 00:15:29,529
versus world, plausibility auditing,
interpretive judgment and fluency trap,

325
00:15:29,579 --> 00:15:31,748
and say why its look-alike is wrong.

326
00:15:31,798 --> 00:15:34,600
Hold the answer until I reply, then grade
me.

327
00:15:34,950 --> 00:15:37,028
Key Terms: The Skeptic's Toolkit.

328
00:15:37,078 --> 00:15:38,730
At Nik Bear Brown.
